# What this package may never name, anywhere under lib/ and test/.
#
# THE TEST FOR A WORD: could a vendor with the same tools and a completely different product still
# use this package with that word in it? The tools of this package are the Dart toolchain, YAML and
# the framework's own types — a registry, a step, a program file — and a name one of those gives its
# own things passes and stays off this list. A name belonging to one product built with them fails
# and goes on it.
#
# THIS PACKAGE JUDGES OTHER PEOPLE'S TREES, which is why it carries a list of its own. A name that
# leaks in here travels to every package that runs these audits, without any of them writing it
# down, and no scan outside this repository reads these files.
#
# One word per line. Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored; everything else is matched
# case-insensitively and word-anchored, so a longer name that merely contains a word is no
# occurrence of it. A COMPOUND IS ITS OWN WORD for that reason: an organisation written one way in
# a package name and another way in a repository URL needs a line for each spelling, which is why
# two of the names below stand here twice, once bare and once with the ending a URL gives it.

# The organisation behind this package and the products it builds, in each form a path, a package
# name or a repository URL writes them.
digita
digitaplatform
hostyour
simetrix
simetrixch

# Those products' choices: the distribution they run their clusters on, the components they deploy,
# the vendors they rent from, and the name they gave their certificate issuer. None of these is a
# tool of this package, which reads programs and drives steps against a fake machine and never a
# real one, so such a word can only arrive here as an example somebody had in mind — which is the
# leak this scan is for.
microk8s
argocd
tekton
zot
authentik
cloudflare
hetzner
letsencrypt-prod

# Those products' own domain words. Forbidden even where one of them reads as ordinary English:
# this is a byte scan and cannot tell one product's entity from the plain noun, so the word is off
# limits here and the sentence says "caller", or "the package that depends on this one", instead.
tenant
consumer
